In first-order <set> theory a <class in set theory> is a definable collection, written schematically $\{x:\theta(x,\vec p)\}$ using a <logical formula> and <set> parameters. A <class term> is an abbreviation for such a defining condition; it does not add a new kind of object to the <quantifiers> of <ZF>. A <class in set theory> is proper if it is not the collection of elements of any <set>.

Define the <cumulative hierarchy> by <transfinite recursion>:
$$
V_0=\varnothing,\qquad V_{\alpha+1}=\mathcal P(V_\alpha),\qquad
V_\lambda=\bigcup_{\alpha<\lambda}V_\alpha\quad(\lambda\text{ a nonzero limit ordinal}),
$$
and let $V=\{x:\exists\alpha\text{ ordinal }(x\in V_\alpha)\}$. Without <foundation> this denotes the well-founded part produced by the hierarchy; it must not simply be assumed to contain every ambient <set>. <Transfinite recursion> on <ordinals> and the relevant <set> constructions remain available in <ZF without foundation>.

Prove transitivity by <transfinite induction>. The empty stage is transitive. Suppose $T=V_\alpha$ is transitive. Then each member of $T$ is a <subset> of $T$, so $T\subseteq\mathcal P(T)$. Also $\mathcal P(T)$ is transitive: if $u\in v\in\mathcal P(T)$, then $u\in T$ and, by transitivity of $T$, $u\subseteq T$, so $u\in\mathcal P(T)$. At a limit stage, a <union> of <transitive sets> is transitive. This proves transitivity at every stage.

Finally transitivity of $V_\alpha$ itself implies that every member is a <subset> of $V_\alpha$, giving
$$
\boxed{V_\alpha\subseteq\mathcal P(V_\alpha)=V_{\alpha+1}.}
$$
These successive inclusions and the limit definition also imply $V_\alpha\subseteq V_\beta$ whenever $\alpha\le\beta$. The argument has not used <foundation>. This leaf includes the unnumbered definitions preceding the source's three claims.

= ii
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For every $x\in a$, the assumption $a\subseteq V$ supplies an <ordinal> stage containing $x$. Let $\alpha_x$ be the least such <ordinal>. Leastness makes this a uniquely definable assignment, so ambient <replacement> gives the <set>
$$
S=\{\alpha_x:x\in a\}.
$$
Put $\beta=\sup S$, taking $\beta=0$ if $a$ is empty. Monotonicity of the hierarchy gives $x\in V_{\alpha_x}\subseteq V_\beta$ for every $x\in a$. Hence $a\subseteq V_\beta$, and therefore
$$
\boxed{a\in\mathcal P(V_\beta)=V_{\beta+1}\subseteq V.}
$$
This is a rank-bounding argument: a set-sized collection of elements from the hierarchy has its stages bounded by one <ordinal>. No simultaneous arbitrary choices are required, since each least stage is uniquely specified.

= iii
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Fix a <logical formula> $\theta(x,y,\vec p)$ with parameters in $V$, and a <set> $a\in V$. Suppose $\langle V,\in\rangle$ satisfies that each $x\in a$ has a unique $y$ with $\theta(x,y,\vec p)$. Relativize every unbounded <quantifier> in $\theta$ to the definable <class in set theory> $V$, obtaining an ambient <first-order formula> $\theta^V$.

Transitivity of $V$ ensures that every member of $a$ belongs to $V$. The ambient relation
$$
y\in V\ \wedge\ \theta^V(x,y,\vec p)
$$
therefore defines a unique ambient <set> $y$ for every $x\in a$. Apply ambient <replacement> to obtain its range <set> $b$. By construction every element of $b$ belongs to $V$, so part (ii) gives $b\in V$.

Membership in this <set> $b$ is exactly the range relation required by <replacement> inside the structure $\langle V,\in\rangle$. Since the <logical formula> and parameters were arbitrary, **every instance of the <replacement> scheme holds in the hierarchy <class in set theory>**. The proof uses <formula relativization to a class> and the <closure> property from part (ii), not the unsupported assertion that $V$ is the whole ambient universe.

